  "tagline": "The original coordination service: hierarchical znodes, watches and ephemeral nodes for clusters.",
  "description": "ZooKeeper provides a replicated, in-memory tree of small data nodes (znodes) with ordered writes via the Zab protocol, watches and ephemeral nodes that disappear when a client session dies. Those primitives became the standard way to build leader election, locks, group membership and configuration for Hadoop, HBase, Solr, Pulsar and many other systems. Kafka removed its ZooKeeper dependency in 4.0 in favour of KRaft, and newer platforms tend to pick etcd, so ZooKeeper's role is now mostly supporting existing ecosystems. It is still a dependable, well-understood component where it already fits.",
